01_05_03_49_59	Debug: {'system_prompt': '\n        You are a Fight The Landlord (Dou Dizhu) player. \n        ### Introduction\n        Fight the Landlord (Dou Dizhu) is a poker climbing game primarily for three players.\n        ### Players, Cards and Deal\n        This game uses a 54-card pack including two jokers, red and black. The cards rank from high to low:\n        red joker, black joker, 2, A, K, Q, J, 10, 9, 8, 7, 6, 5, 4, 3. Suits are irrelevant.\n\n        ### Play\n        The landlord plays first, and may play a single card or any legal combination.\n        Each subsequent player in anticlockwise order must either pass (play no card) or beat the previous play\n        by playing a higher combination of the same number of cards and same type.\n        There are just two exceptions to this: a rocket can beat any combination, and a bomb can beat any combination\n        except a higher bomb or rocket.\n\n        ### Combinations\n        1. Single card: ranking from three (low) up to red joker (high).\n        2. Pair: two cards of the same rank, from three (low) up to two (high).\n        3. Triplet: three cards of the same rank.\n        4. Triplet with an attached card: a triplet with any single card added.\n        5. Triplet with an attached pair: a triplet with a pair added.\n        6. Sequence: at least five cards of consecutive rank, from 3 up to ace. Twos and jokers cannot be used.\n        7. Sequence of pairs: at least three pairs of consecutive ranks, from 3 up to ace. Twos and jokers cannot be used.\n        8. Sequence of triplets: at least two triplets of consecutive ranks from three up to ace.\n        9. Sequence of triplets with attached cards: an extra card is added to each triplet.\n        10. Sequence of triplets with attached pairs: an extra pair is attached to each triplet.\n        11. Bomb: four cards of the same rank. Beats everything except a rocket and a higher bomb.\n        12. Rocket: a pair of jokers. The highest combination, beats everything.\n        13. Quadplex set: a quad with two single cards OR two pairs attached.\n        Use the EXACT card symbols provided in the Valid Moves list.\n        Prepare a valid JSON object like {"cards": ["♠3", "♥3"]} for your answer, along with your explanation and reason. If you pass, output: {"cards": []}\n        \n        Now is your turn, to your best ability, choose your move to maximize your chances of winning.\n        Please briefly evaluate a few proimsing moves, provide reason for your final choice, and output the action with specified format.\n        Your response format should be:\n        Evaluation: [Your Evaluation]\n        Reason: [Your Reason]\n        Answer: [Your Answer]\n    ', 'user_prompt': "You are playing as a peasant. Your aim is to be the play out all your cards before the landlord does, OR help your teammate (the other peasant) play out their cards before the landlord does. If the landlord plays out all cards first, you lose.\nYou are a Peasant. The Next Player (Right) is the Landlord (ENEMY). The Previous Player (Left) is your TEAMMATE (Peasant). You must cooperate with your teammate to beat the Landlord. The objective is to empty your hand before the landlord does, or help your teammate empty their hand before the landlord does.\n\nYour Hand: ♣3 ♦3 ♠5 ♣5 ♠6 ♥6 ♦6 ♣9 ♦9 ♣10 ♠J ♣J ♣Q ♦Q ♣K ♦A ♥2\nLast Play to Beat: ♠4 ♥4 ♣4 ♠7 ♥7\n--- Game History (Recent) ---\nTurn 1 (Next Player): ♠4 ♥4 ♣4 ♠7 ♥7\n--- Player Info ---\nCards Held by Next Player (Right) [Landlord (Opponent)]: 15\nCards Held by Prev Player (Left) [Peasant (Teammate)]: 17\n--- Cards Remaining Unknown (Card Counting) ---\n(Format explanation: 'Rank: Count', e.g., '2: 4' means there are four 2s left unknown)\nRedJoker: 1, BlackJoker: 1, 2: 3, A: 3, K: 3, Q: 2, J: 2, 10: 3, 9: 2, 8: 4, 7: 2, 6: 1, 5: 2, 4: 1, 3: 2\nValid Moves:\ntriple_with_pair: ♣3 ♦3 ♠6 ♥6 ♦6\ntriple_with_pair: ♠5 ♣5 ♠6 ♥6 ♦6\ntriple_with_pair: ♠6 ♥6 ♦6 ♣9 ♦9\ntriple_with_pair: ♠6 ♥6 ♦6 ♠J ♣J\ntriple_with_pair: ♠6 ♥6 ♦6 ♣Q ♦Q\npass: []\n\nSelect the best move from the Valid Moves list above. You can choose to pass even if you have valid moves that can beat the previous play, but you cannot pass if you are in a Free Play (no last play).\nEnsure your response matches one of the valid moves exactly.\nRemember to provide Explanation, Reason, and Answer.", 'reasoning': "**Decision to Counter the Landlord's Play**\n\nOkay, here's the situation: the Landlord, who's to my right, just played a full house, 444+77. My teammate to my left, I think, passed. This means it's my turn. I've got a hand heavy on pairs and a single triplet, 666. Looking at my hand, I've got a clear move: I need to use this triplet. I'm thinking of playing my 666 triplet, paired with a small card, 33, to beat the Landlord's move. This is my only triplet. If I don't use it now, it's pretty much useless. Plus, 33 is small and won't be valuable later. If I play this, it forces the Landlord to respond. Even if they beat it with a higher full house, I've burned one of their high cards and it prevents them from free-playing again. If they have to respond to 666+33, or 666+55, then I benefit regardless. It's a risk, because they could have something bigger, but it's the right play. I'm going to play 666 paired with 33. It's the best strategic move in this scenario. I am going to play ♣3 ♦3 ♠6 ♥6 ♦6.\n\n\n", 'output': 'Evaluation: The Landlord (Next Player) has opened with a Full House (444+77). I have a slightly higher Full House (666+33) that can beat this. Since 666 is a low triplet, it is unlikely to be effective later in the game against higher hands. Playing it now allows me to make use of it while also acting as a blocker to the Landlord\'s control. I should attach my lowest pair (33) to preserve higher pairs (55, 99, JJ, QQ) for defense. Passing would allow the Landlord to win the trick (since my teammate presumably passed) and lead freely, which is dangerous.\n\nReason: My hand consists primarily of pairs and a single triplet (666). The Landlord\'s 444-77 is a strong control play. Beating it with 666-33 is the most efficient move, utilizing my lowest pair and clearing my only triplet which is otherwise vulnerable. This forces the Landlord to use a high-value hand to regain control or pass the initiative to my teammate/me.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♣3", "♦3", "♠6", "♥6", "♦6"]}'}
